                title: "Where does an operation read old values from?",
                source: "unified-surface-plan.md sec. 13.2 (PR #239); brief U-1",
                description: r#"An edit needs the old value to be undoable (`place` needs the old pose). Scripts get it today from `SceneView`, a copy of the scene taken once per run, because a Steel builtin cannot hold `&World`.

The first plan moved that snapshot into the new bottom crate `gradiance-op`. It cannot go there: it holds `ShapeDef` and `BodyPhysics`, which live in crates above."#,
                decision: true,
                options: [
                    (
                        label: "Mirrors in a context",
                        caption: r#"Recommended. `gradiance-op` defines `OpContext`, a container of read-only mirrors, and names none of them. `gradiance-command` owns the scene snapshot; `ui` owns `PanelStates`. Every doorway fills the context with one builder. Cost: the snapshot grows when an operation needs a new old value, as it does today."#,
                    ),
                    (
                        label: "Read the World directly",
                        caption: r#"`lower(params, &World)`. No snapshot to keep current. But the Lisp doorway must then queue its calls and lower them after the script ends, and query verbs still need the snapshot to answer at their call site. Two read paths exist, and a bad argument is reported at the end of the run, not at its line."#,
                    ),
                ],
            ),
